[Course "The Traxler Counterattack"]
[ChesslyCourse "72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b"]
[CourseColor "black"]
[CourseDescription "One of the BEST openings for Black against White's very famous Fried Liver Attack. You will win many games in under 15 moves with massive attacks on your opponent's king."]
[Chapter "The Traxler Main Line (6.Kxf2)"]
[Study "6.Kxf2 Lines"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Traxler Counterattack/01-01 The Traxler Main Line (6.Kxf2) - 6.Kxf2 Lines.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{Welcome to the Traxler! This is an aggressive gambit against the Fried Liver Attack. There are more than 25 million games played in the Fried Liver, so the chances you'll get to play your Traxler are really high! White starts with 1.e4. We'll play 1...e5 to lay claim to the center as well.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 {White attacks our e5-pawn. Let's defend our center. [%csl Re5]} 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 {White goes for the Italian Game. We'll develop our other knight, because we're not afraid of the Fried Liver.} 3... Nf6 4. Ng5 {The Fried Liver Attack. White converges on f7. What can we do? We can go for a counterattack! This is a gambit of our f7-pawn, but if White takes, they can get into a world of trouble. [%csl Rf7]} 4... Bc5 5. Nxf7 {About 63% of games go like this. White cannot resist forking us. But this is actually a mistake. We take their f-pawn with check! This is The Traxler! The point is not material, but the fact that we'll be immediately attacking the king. [%csl Rh8,Rd8]} 5... Bxf2+ 6. Kxf2 {Close to 60% of games feature 6.Kxf2, which is why we're seeing this first in our course. Since the king is vulnerable, we'll continue giving checks with aggression. Our knight jumps in, capturing the e-pawn! The white king won't know what to do. A good rule to remember is that the king loses by going to any light square! [%csl Re4]} 6... Nxe4+ 7. Kg1 {The most popular king move is the safest. 7.Kg1 happens in about 30% of games. The king is safe from checks for now. Our queen will arrive on h4 and threaten mate on f2! [%csl Rf2]} (7. Ke3 {The other move that does not lose, but that also seems to be not so unpopular, is 7.Ke3. White plays this about 28% of the time, which is north of 270,000 games. White is in serious trouble after we play Qh4! Stockfish can defend this, but 99% of the people online aren't Stockfish! [%csl Re3]} 7... Qh4 8. Nxh8 {The knight takes on h8. This leads to a beautiful finish. We have a forced checkmate, starting with Qf4+. [%csl Rf4]} (8. Qf3 {This move doesn't work. Retreating our attacked knight wins the game. Our plan is ...Nd4 to continue the checkmate attack.} 8... Nf6 9. Nxh8 {So, let's say the knight takes our rook.} 9... Nd4 {We can attack the queen. The attack on the queen is one thing, but the point is there are so many pieces around White's king. 10.Qg3 Nf5+ is a nasty fork. [%cal Gf3g3,Gd4f5] [%csl Rf3]}) (8. g3 {This move is the best, but White has to find it. We can take on g3. Very important: with the idea of ...Qd4+ because ...Qxc4 isn't our goal. We want to attack the king. Material is out of the question for now. [%csl Rg3]} 8... Nxg3 9. hxg3 {White recaptures. And we give a check to force the king to a bad file.} 9... Qd4+ 10. Kf3 {On the f-file, we can do things with tempo.} 10... O-O {White's pieces are hanging. Believe it or not, the only move that doesn't lose here is 11.Rh4! We would then continue with e4+ and a wild position ensues which the engine evaluates as approximately equal. Your opponents are not engines though! [%cal Gh1h4] [%csl Gf8,Rf7]}) 8... Qf4+ 9. Kd3 {The king goes to d3. We'll play Nb4+ and force the king backwards. Mate is near.} 9... Nb4+ 10. Ke2 {The only move.} 10... Qf2# {Checkmate. Many finishes like this are possible if White messes up! [%csl Re2]}) (7. Ke1 {This is a terrible move: White is putting the pieces back on the home squares to play another game. This happens in roughly 26% of games. Qh4+ is a lethal check. Since the king is on a bad square, g3 doesn't work.} 7... Qh4+ 8. g3 {White blocks.} 8... Nxg3 {We have a winning attack. We're threatening a discovered check on the king. White can't even take our knight as the rook would fall with check and then more material will go afterwards. [%csl Rh1,Re1]}) 7... Qh4 8. g3 {A simple way of defending is 8.g3, which has been seen in close to 50,000 games. White can defend the f2-square with the queen, of course. But 8.Qf3 or Qf1 is met with 8...Rf8 and suddenly the knight on f7 is pinned! ...d5 and ...Nd4 would also cause problems. We'll take on g3! Retreating is not an option! We have a draw at the very least, and a checkmate at best! [%csl Rg3]} 8... Nxg3 9. hxg3 {The pawn recaptures. This move is the most popular. We'll take on g3 and give check.} (9. Nxh8 {A lower percentage of games go like this. But we don't care about material in the Traxler: we care about attacking the white king! Our queen's knight leaps into the center.} 9... Nd4 10. hxg3 {White is advised to capture on g3 now. We'll recapture with check. [%csl Rg3]} 10... Qxg3+ 11. Kf1 {The king moves away.} 11... Qf4+ {The best thing we have here is a draw by repetition, if White can find all the correct moves, of course! [%csl Rf1]}) 9... Qxg3+ 10. Kf1 {The king is forced to f1.} 10... Rf8 {And we can pin the knight on f7. We have ideas of d5 to distract the bishop on c4 but also to play Bh3+. White has to pray for a draw. [%cal Gd7d5,Gc8h3] [%csl Rf7]} *

[Chapter "White Doesn't Take The Bishop (6.Kf1)"]
[Study "6.Kf1 Lines"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Traxler Counterattack/02-01 White Doesn't Take The Bishop (6.Kf1) - 6.Kf1 Lines.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{In this study you'll learn how to crush people with the Traxler in the positions that may arise after we take on f2 and White goes 6.Kf1. White starts off in the most traditional way, pushing the king's pawn in the center. We'll mirror White's opening with 1...e5.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 {White develops the knight, targeting the e5 pawn. We'll develop our knight as well, defending e5. [%csl Re5]} 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 {The Italian game, one of the most popular openings at any level. We develop our other knight to f6, attacking the e4-pawn. [%csl Re4]} 3... Nf6 4. Ng5 {White decides to enter the Fried Liver! ...Bc5 is The Traxler counterattack! [%csl Rf7]} 4... Bc5 5. Nxf7 {This is our second study looking at 5.Nxf7. It looks like we have too many pieces hanging, but we'll sacrifice another to put the White king in danger: Bxf2+! [%csl Rh8,Rd8]} 5... Bxf2+ 6. Kf1 {In this chapter we will analyze the positions arising from Kf1, which is the best move, but White's position remains in peril. It's not the most common move, however, played 41% of the time, which is 662 thousand games. White is using our bishop as a shield. Let's go ahead and remove our queen from danger.} 6... Qe7 7. Nxh8 {White decides to go up a rook by capturing on h8. Now that we we have sacrificed the rook, let's focus on attacking the king! 7...d5 opens the position even more. Our light-squared bishop is unleashed with a tempo! [%csl Gc8]} 7... d5 8. exd5 {White recaptures and gains a tempo on our knight. We'll jump into the center with our knight, with the threat of Bg4, trapping White's queen.} (8. Bxd5 {White has missed the point of 7...d5. This move blunders the queen.} 8... Bg4 {And we trap the queen! [%csl Rd1]}) 8... Nd4 9. c3 {9.c3 is a critical move, because it opens an escape diagonal for the queen in case of Bg4 and creates a threat on the knight. Bg4 is still a very strong developing move. [%cal Gd1a4] [%csl Rd4]} (9. d3 {d3 is the most played move. It opens the dark-squared bishop and creates an escape on d2 for the white queen. Bg4 is still a very strong developing move. [%csl Gc1]} 9... Bg4 10. Qd2 {Qd2 is the only move which prevents the loss of the queen. Now our f2 bishop is really threatened! Now's the time to save that bishop. Bh4 opens up the f-file for future action. [%csl Rf2]} 10... Bh4 11. c3 {White decides to attack our knight pushing the c-pawn. But now we can jump into e4 with the knight, attacking the queen! Of course White can take... [%csl Rd4]} 11... Ne4 12. dxe4 {But now the door's wide open! Let's go in. Qf6+, and we force White into a corner.} 12... Qf6+ 13. Kg1 {Danger levels! Pause and try to find the move for black! With Ne2+, we block the queen's vision of the f2 square. [%csl Rf2]} 13... Ne2+ 14. Bxe2 {White had to take with the queen to avoid immediate checkmate.} 14... Qf2# {Checkmate in 14 moves! [%csl Rg1]}) (9. Kxf2 {Maybe White chooses to take the bishop now. We can land a check. This time we'll put the knight on g4.} 9... Ng4+ 10. Ke1 {Choosing to retreat to e1. But Qf6, threatening mate, causes problems down the f-file. [%csl Rf2]} (10. Kg1 {If White corners themselves...} 10... Qc5 {We threaten a deadly discovery, and if the king runs to the f-file, Qf8+ is lethal. [%cal Rc5g1]}) 10... Qf6 11. Rf1 {White obviously tries to prevent this... We'll check on h4.} 11... Qh4+ 12. g3 {White blocks it, but... [%csl Rh4]} 12... Qxh2 {We can capture the recently undefended h2-pawn and continue with a vicious attack against the king. [%cal Gh2g3] [%csl Re1]}) (9. d6 {This continuation is White's best line. It's a key defensive resource, giving White access to f7. We'll take the pawn with our queen. [%csl Rf7]} 9... Qxd6 10. Nf7 {White brings their knight out with a tempo. We'll deploy the queen to c5, where it is lined up against f2. It also attacks the white bishop.} 10... Qc5 11. d3 {White defends it. We develop our last minor piece, attacking the white queen. [%cal Gd3c4]} 11... Bg4 12. Qd2 {White moves it to safety, attacking our bishop. [%csl Rf2]} 12... Bh4 {We retreat! White still has a lot of problems to solve. The immediate issues are ...Be2+ and ...b5. Keep in mind you don't have to memorize the entirety of this continuation, but merely understand the ideas within this system.}) 9... Bg4 10. Qa4+ {White saves their queen while giving a check. Let's calmly block the check, while potentially freeing up the f6 square for our queen. The idea of opening up the f-file is very important.} 10... Nd7 11. cxd4 {A common blunder... We are currently down 9 points of material, but our attack is crushing.} (11. Kxf2 {11.Kxf2 is by far the funniest line in the chapter... just that only we get a laugh! The White king is left exposed on f2. Now 11...Qh4+ is a very tricky check. [%csl Rf2]} 11... Qh4+ 12. g3 {White tries to block the check with the pawn, hoping to create a shield. Now the light squares around the king are really weak. We switch back with the queen. Qf6+ basically guarantees the win for Black. Now White is forced to the back rank. [%csl Rf3,Re4]} (12. Ke3 {If the king tries to run, desperatly hoping for an escape via d3-c2... Qh6+ is a powerful check which forces the king back.} 12... Qh6+ 13. Kf2 {Kf2 is forced. Now Qf4+ comes with devastating effects. The king is forced onto the back rank!} 13... Qf4+ 14. Kg1 {The king is cornered. Now Ne2+ and the knight must be taken by the bishop! We'll win the queen next move!} 14... Ne2+ 15. Bxe2 {The bishop has to take. It's the only legal move.} 15... Qxa4 {White has lost the queen, and will probably have a tough time developing that queenside. By the time they manage to move, you should be able to mate the king or simply gain more material. [%csl Rc1,Rb1,Ra1]}) 12... Qf6+ 13. Ke1 {Notice how our bishop prevents the White king from escaping to the queenside. [%csl Gg4,Rd1]} 13... Qf5 {Qf5! A slow move that secures the advantage: we're infiltrating on the light squares. [%cal Gf5e4,Gd4c2]}) 11... Qf6 {Qf6 sets up a deadly discovered check. White now can only delay checkmate with ridiculous moves giving away material. [%cal Gf6f1] [%csl Gf6,Rf1]} *

[Chapter "White Tames The Traxler (5.Bxf7+)"]
[Study "5.Bxf7+ Lines"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Traxler Counterattack/03-01 White Tames The Traxler (5.Bxf7+) - 5.Bxf7+ Lines.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{In this chapter, we'll analyze a very sound continuation for White in the Traxler counterattack: namely, when White plays 5.Bxf7+. This is considered better for White than the knight capture, but let's see how we can complicate matters! White opens with the king's pawn. We'll mirror their choice.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 {White develops, attacking e5. We'll bring our knight out and support our center. [%csl Re5]} 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 {White develops, with an eye on f7. We'll develop and attack the e4-pawn. [%csl Re4]} 3... Nf6 4. Ng5 {White defends e4 and converges on f7. But in the Traxler, we ignore the incoming attack and target f2! [%csl Rf7]} 4... Bc5 5. Bxf7+ {White's safest continuation is to take on f7 with the bishop, checking our king. We've got to move up with the king. [%csl Re8]} 5... Ke7 6. Bd5 {Bd5 offers to create trades when we are down material and without castling rights! It's played 17% of the time, same as Bc4. Let's place our rook opposite the f2 pressure point! [%csl Rf2]} (6. Bb3 {This move ensures that the bishop survives instead of proposing a trade. It's also good because it controls diagonals near our king! It's by far the most common move, played more than half of the time. We get ready to activate our rook on the f-file!} 6... Rf8 7. O-O {White gets to safety. We prepare to develop our bishop. [%csl Gc8]} 7... d6 8. Nc3 {White goes for piece development. Let's proceed with our idea of activating the queen.} 8... Qe8 9. Nd5+ {White gives a check! We'll retreat, defending c7. [%csl Re7,Rc7]} 9... Kd8 10. d4 {This move, going for development, gifts us a pawn. Let's take it. [%csl Rd4]} (10. c3 {c3 seems sound, preparing d4. Let's force the knight away! [%cal Gd2d4]} 10... h6 11. d4 {White can choose to ignore and push in the center! We'll take! [%csl Rd4]} 11... exd4 12. Nxf6 {White trades knights. We'll recapture with the rook, as we already have a pawn on h6 chasing the knight from g5. [%csl Rg5]} 12... Rxf6 13. e5 {After the clever e5, opening up the position because we have our king in the center...} 13... Rf5 {Moving our rook. Attacking the knight and the pawn gets us out of trouble. [%cal Rf5e5,Rh6g5]}) (10. h3 {This move controls g4 for White. But we can push the knight away. [%csl Rg4]} 10... h6 11. Nxf6 {And if White captures our knight. We'll recapture with the rook.} 11... Rxf6 12. Nf3 {After Nf3, retreating...} 12... Qh5 {Our queen enters the kingside, applying intense pressure to the position. [%csl Rf3,Rh3]}) 10... Nxd4 11. Nxf6 {White liquidates, but... This allows us to recapture, attacking the knight.} 11... gxf6 12. Nf3 {White retreats, but runs into...} 12... Bg4 {A pin! Suddenly the white king doesn't look so safe. [%cal Rd4f3] [%csl Rg4,Rf3,Re2,Rd1]}) (6. Bc4 {Finally, Bc4 retreats without allowing a trade, and also has defensive ideas. White players opt for this move 17% of the time. We can proceed with ideas of bringing our queen into the kingside to apply pressure there.} 6... Qe8 7. O-O {White gets to safety. We prepare to unleash our bishop. [%csl Gc8]} 7... d6 8. c3 {White prepares d4. We'll bring our rook to the semi-open f-file. [%cal Gd2d4]} 8... Rf8 9. Nf3 {White retreats preemptively. And so do we.} 9... Bb6 10. d4 {White strikes in the center.} 10... Qg6 {But we have a kingside attack in our plans! [%cal Gc8g4] [%csl Rf3]}) 6... Rf8 7. O-O {White is well advised in getting their king to safety. Now we unleash our bishop, with ideas of h6 followed by a pin when White goes Nf3.} 7... d6 8. h3 {h3 controls g4 so our pieces cannot access that square. Let's remove that pesky knight from our position. [%csl Rg4]} 8... h6 9. Nf3 {White retreats. We're down a pawn material, so we're looking to activate our pieces! We plan to bring the queen to g6.} 9... Qe8 10. c3 {White continues with regular central play, as they should. Let's sacrifice on h3 to weaken the kingside!} 10... Bxh3 11. gxh3 {White recaptures. Now our queen arrives on g6 with check!} 11... Qg6+ 12. Kh1 {White can choose to escape here. We'll capture on d5 in order to open up squares for our queen. [%csl Rd5]} (12. Kh2 {This move isn't as good. We can trade in the center, opening up our rook! [%csl Gf8,Rd5]} 12... Nxd5 13. exd5 {After White recaptures...} (13. d4 {White can choose to open up the position, and unleash their dark-squared bishop. But we can take the e-pawn and attack the f3-knight. [%csl Re4]} 13... Qxe4 14. Nbd2 {After White develops, defending f3 and attacking our queen...} 14... Qf4+ {We can sidestep with check. [%csl Rh2]}) 13... Qd3 {We can play the very powerful Qd3, controlling white's position, and attacking the knight on f3! [%csl Rf3]}) 12... Nxd5 13. exd5 {White recaptures and attacks our knight. Let's apply pressure to the f3-knight and prevent White from developing with d4. [%csl Rf3]} (13. d4 {This move attacks our bishop and center, while giving White's dark-squared bishop scope. But we can pin the knight! [%csl Rf3]} 13... Qxe4 14. Nd2 {White develops, attacking and defending. We retreat to f5, viewing h3. Note that the white bishop is no longer covering f4. [%csl Rh3]} 14... Qf5 15. dxc5 {White takes our bishop.} 15... Qxh3+ {But we can take on h3, giving a check, and the game continues. The position is tricky and we have good practical chances despite the material deficit. [%csl Rh1]}) 13... Qd3 14. Nh2 {If White removes the knight from danger.. We'll take on f2 with our bishop! [%csl Rf2]} 14... Bxf2 15. Qg4 {White may try to bring another piece to the kingside, both defending and attacking! We'll play Rf4. The idea of this clever move is to vacate f8 for our other rook!} 15... Rf4 16. Qxg7+ {White takes with check! We can block with tempo, and f8 is now vacated. [%csl Re7]} 16... Rf7 17. Qg2 {White retreats, attacking the bishop. We bring another rook into the mix, and protect f2. [%csl Rf2]} 17... Raf8 18. dxc6 {White can take our knight, but... We can retreat and ignore this, as our power on the f-file is great! [%csl Gf7,Gf8]} 18... Bb6 19. Rxf7+ {White trades a pair of rooks. We'll recapture, obviously.} 19... Rxf7 20. cxb7 {White gets a step close to queening their pawn. But we'll harass White's queen with our rook.} 20... Rf2 21. Qg7+ {White checks us... [%csl Re7]} 21... Rf7 {But we block, basically offering a repetition. Should White choose to continue by taking our rook and queening on b8, we are able to mate the exposed king!} *

[Chapter "Traxler Early Sidelines"]
[Study "White's Other 5th Move Options"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Traxler Counterattack/04-01 Traxler Early Sidelines - White's Other 5th Move Options.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{This is the fourth study of the Traxler. Previously, we saw Bxf7+ on move 5. But what if White goes for something else on move 5, like castling, d4 trying to open the position up, d3 being timid, or just development with Nc3? White opens with the king's pawn. We mirror White's choice.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 {White attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn. [%csl Re5]} 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 {White develops with an eye on f7. We'll develop, attacking e4. [%csl Rf7,Re4]} 3... Nf6 4. Ng5 {White defends e4 and converges on f7! But we'll ignore the incoming attack and target f2! [%csl Rf7]} 4... Bc5 5. d4 {White chooses to break in the center! But we have a move of our own! This move is played 1% of the time, but is extremely confrontational, so we need to learn how to play against it! Let's push with d5, attacking the bishop!} (5. O-O {White chooses safety before the attack! Around 6% of players don't want to get into the tricky Traxler lines. That's about 200,000 games. We'll also castle. We're now guarding f7! [%csl Rf7]} 5... O-O 6. d3 {White also castles. We'll prepare to develop our bishop.} 6... d6 7. Nc3 {White develops a knight. And we kick the other knight back.} 7... h6 8. Nf3 {After they retreat... We'll create a pin. [%csl Rf3]} 8... Bg4 9. h3 {White tries to remove the pin. But we can just maintain it. [%csl Rg4]} 9... Bh5 10. g4 {And g4 is a mistake. We can sacrifice a piece and maintain the pin. [%csl Rg4]} 10... Nxg4 11. hxg4 {White takes the knight.} 11... Bxg4 {But the pin is revived and our knight can even jump to d4 next move to attack the pinned knight. [%cal Gc6d4,Gd8f6] [%csl Gg4,Rf3,Rd1]}) (5. d3 {White attempts to be solid, supporting their center with d3. 5.d3 is only played 1% of the time. But we can get our king to safety and then question White's knight on g5.} 5... O-O 6. O-O {White also castles. We'll prepare to develop our bishop.} 6... d6 7. c3 {White plays c3, in preparation of d4. Let's kick the knight out. [%cal Gd3d4] [%csl Rg5]} 7... h6 8. Nf3 {The knight retreats... And we'll pin it to the queen. [%csl Rf3]} 8... Bg4 9. h3 {White attacks us. We'll maintain the pin.} 9... Bh5 10. g4 {White cannot stubbornly remove the pin. We'll simply hit them with the knight sacrifice on g4. [%csl Rg4]} 10... Nxg4 11. hxg4 {After recapturing...} 11... Bxg4 {We can maintain the pin to much success. [%csl Gg4,Rf3,Rd1]}) (5. Nc3 {White goes for piece development, but it's questionable whether this is what the position requires. This move is only played 1% of the time and doesn't seem natural, because it gives us time to castle, which we'll do straightaway!} 5... O-O 6. d3 {White pushes d3. We'll push d6 ourselves.} 6... d6 7. Nd5 {White offers a minor piece trade. But we'll aim to trade off White's good bishop on c4. [%csl Rc4]} 7... Na5 8. Nxf6+ {If White continues trading down...} 8... Qxf6 {We develop the queen while attacking f2. Next, we can push the knight back with ...h6, or seize the bishop pair by capturing on c4. [%cal Gh7h6,Ga5c4] [%csl Rf2]}) 5... d5 6. exd5 {Should White take the pawn, attacking our knight... We'll get the knight out of danger and remove the attacker of our bishop, too. [%csl Rc6]} (6. dxc5 {Should White take our bishop... We'll take theirs. [%csl Rc4]} 6... dxc4 7. Qxd8+ {And after the trade...} 7... Nxd8 {We recapture with the knight and have an equal endgame.}) (6. Bxd5 {The bishop can also take. We'll give an intermezzo check.} 6... Bb4+ 7. c3 {White blocks it. We'll take the bishop on d5. [%csl Rb4]} 7... Nxd5 8. exd5 {White recaptures. But this allows us to activate our queen.} 8... Qxd5 9. cxb4 {White takes our bishop.} 9... Qxg2 {But we take on g2, attacking the rook! White can easily go wrong here. Qf3 is the only real defense, and the queens will come off. But we'll still be causing trouble with moves such as ...e4 and Nxb4, giving us active compensation for our piece deficit. [%cal Ge5d4,Gc6b4] [%csl Rh1,Rc2]}) 6... Nxd4 7. O-O {White gets to safety, but... We can develop with a tempo on the queen.} 7... Bg4 8. Qd2 {White attempts to move out of danger. But we'll kick the knight. [%csl Rg5]} 8... h6 9. Nh3 {And once they retreat the knight... We can take it. [%csl Rh3]} 9... Bxh3 10. gxh3 {If White recaptures...} 10... Nf3+ {That's a fork. We win White's queen! [%csl Rd2,Rg1]} *

[Chapter "Italian Game Sidelines"]
[Study "White Plays 4.d3"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/The Traxler Counterattack/05-01 Italian Game Sidelines - White Plays 4.d3.mp4"]
[Orientation "black"]

{In this chapter we'll go over some sidelines in the Italian Game. In particular, what if we can't employ our Traxler because white doesn't go for the Fried Liver attack? In this study, we'll see what to do if White chooses to simply defend the e4 pawn with 4.d3. We begin with White's opening 1.e4. We begin with White's opening 1.e4, and we'll reply with ...e5.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 {White develops, attacking e5. We'll develop, defending the e-pawn. [%csl Re5]} 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 {White brings the bishop out in standard Italian fashion. We'll bring our knight out, attacking e4. [%csl Re4]} 3... Nf6 4. d3 {In this study we'll break down what to do after the very solid d3, simply defending the pawn with another pawn. This move is played 28% of the time, which is more than 20 million games! In this position I suggest h6! This has two ideas: the defensive one is to take the g5 square away from a white piece. The offensive one is to prepare a g5 push! [%csl Ge4,Gd3]} 4... h6 5. O-O {White can choose to bring their king to safety. And they do so about a quarter of the time. We'll further support the e-pawn by playing ...d6.} (5. c3 {Alternatively, c3 indicates that White would like to push d4. It's played 16% of the time. Thus here we'll support our central pawn and open up our light-squared bishop. [%cal Gd3d4]} 5... d6 6. Nbd2 {White continues their development. Based on how the game is developing, considering White is quite solid, I suggest we play g6, not g5, to secure our bishop on g7. Additionally, g6 controls the f5 square.} 6... g6 7. d4 {White breaks in the center! Let's continue with our plan.} 7... Bg7 8. O-O {White castles... And so will we.} 8... O-O 9. dxe5 {White can choose to take in the center. But we can recapture with the pawn.} 9... dxe5 10. a4 {White proceeds with queenside expansion. We'll bring our queen out, supporting our pawn, while vacating the d8 square for a rook. [%cal Gb2b4]} 10... Qe7 11. b4 {White continues expanding. We'll bring our rook to d8.} 11... Rd8 12. Qc2 {White steps out of danger. Let's mobilize our knight on the kingside. It's headed for f4 with attacking ideas. [%csl Rf4]} 12... Nh5 13. Re1 {White begins establishing a defensive setup. We can transfer our queen to the kingside to apply pressure.} 13... Qf6 14. Nf1 {White brings a knight to f1 in order to have more defenders around their king. We'll attack f3. [%csl Rf3]} 14... Bg4 15. N3d2 {The f3 knight retreats.} 15... Nf4 {Now from this position onwards, we have the idea of playing our bishop back to e6, trading it and recapturing with the queen, in order to infiltrate the kingside with our queen. [%cal Gg4e6,Gf6e6]}) 5... d6 6. c3 {White prepares d4. We'll expand with g5, now that White has castled!} 6... g5 7. Re1 {White supports e4 before pushing d4. This logical move is chosen 18% of the time. Let's continue marching on the kingside! [%cal Gd3d4] [%csl Ge1,Re4]} (7. Nbd2 {White supports e4 with the knight. It's played 12% of the time at this point. Let's just fianchetto. [%csl Ge4,Gd3]} 7... Bg7 {Let's just fianchetto.} 8. Bb3 {White retreats to preserve their bishop against any attacks. Let's get our king to safety.} 8... O-O 9. Nc4 {White goes to the nice c4 square with the knight. This move makes a d5 push slightly more difficult for us. Let's develop, hitting that knight and opposing White's bishop. [%csl Re5]} 9... Be6 10. Re1 {White supports e4 with the rook. Likewise, we align our rook to the defense of e5.} 10... Re8 11. a4 {White expands on the queenside. But now we'll strike in the center with d5!} 11... d5 12. Ncxe5 {The knight gets out of danger and takes our pawn! We take the knight.} 12... Nxe5 13. Nxe5 {White recaptures, as they should. But now we can continue capturing, opening up the position favorably for our pieces.} 13... dxe4 14. d4 {White can advance and defend their knight. 14.dxe4 was impossible because we could take the bishop on b3 first, and then capture the knight on e5. We'll play to undermine the pawn. [%csl Ge5,Gd4]} 14... c5 15. a5 {White ignores c5 and continues pushing. We'll give White an isolated queen's pawn. [%csl Rd4]} 15... cxd4 16. cxd4 {White recaptures, now with the IQP.} 16... Rc8 {Now we take control of the c-file. As is apparent in the current position, Black has good major piece activity. Meanwhile White struggles to find a plan, whereas we can continue with piling up the pressure on the isolated pawn. [%csl Gc8,Rd4]}) 7... g4 8. Nfd2 {White retreats. Our march goes on with h5!} 8... h5 9. Nf1 {White brings pieces for the defensive task. We'll enter the g-file, preventing the bishop from going to g5, and supporting our pawns going forward! [%csl Rg5]} (9. d4 {White can strike in the center! In which case our assault may proceed with ...h4.} 9... h4 10. Nf1 {The knight goes back to defend. After all this time, we'll fianchetto!} 10... Bg7 11. Bg5 {White pins us! Let's attack the white bishop with ...Rh5! [%csl Rf6]} 11... Rh5 12. Qc1 {The queen defends. We'll bring our queen forward in preparation for getting our king to safety.} 12... Qe7 13. Bd5 {White can attempt to trade a piece.} 13... Bd7 {But we'll just develop and be ready to castle queenside. Our kingside attack is already well underway. [%cal Ge8c8] [%csl Gh4,Gg4,Gh5]}) 9... Rg8 10. Be3 {White develops the bishop. We'll continue to expand and play ...h4.} 10... h4 11. g3 {White puts a stop to our advance. But we'll just develop normally.} 11... Bd7 12. b4 {White expands on the queenside. Meanwhile we'll develop the queen and get ready to castle.} 12... Qe7 13. Nbd2 {White concludes developing their queenside minor pieces.} 13... Nd8 {The knight is rerouted with the intent of going to the kingside! In the future, we might play ...Rh8 and ...Bh6 to trade for White's bishop on e3 and place the knight on g5. [%cal Gd8e6,Gf8h6]} *

