{Welcome to the second study of the early sidelines. Presently, we'll look at the Max Lange Attack, which occurs after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6 4.d4! White strikes early in the center with aggressive ideas. Let's see how to counter this early deviation! White begins with 1.e4, and we'll reply in kind.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 {White develops, attacking our pawn. We'll defend with the knight. [%csl Re5]} 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 {The bishop comes out to play. We'll develop, attacking e4. [%csl Re4]} 3... Nf6 4. d4 {And White strikes immediately in the center! This move is played only 7% of the time under 2000 Elo, but that's still almost 5 million games. Here I suggest we take on d4. [%csl Rd4]} 4... exd4 5. e5 {White advances, chasing our knight from f6! This is the most popular move, chosen 38% of the time in this position. How about we chase their bishop away from c4 in return? [%csl Rf6]} (5. O-O {Castling! Offering the central pawns in exchange for activity of the pieces. Expect to face this move 28% of the time. Let's go ahead and take the e-pawn, and see how White wants to proceed. [%csl Re4]} 5... Nxe4 6. Re1 {Re1 is the most logical move, pinning our knight and getting ready to accumulate pressure on it. And you'll have to play against it by far the most often, 73% of the time. In reply, d5 attacks the bishop on c4 and defends our knight! [%csl Re4]} (6. Nc3 {This is the Nakhmanson Gambit! Try not to be greedy, and just simplify to your benefit. You'll encounter this gambit 13% of the time. We will play the safer Nxc3. Taking with the pawn allows crazy stuff from White, like the lines after 7.Bxf7. [%csl Rc3]} 6... Nxc3 7. bxc3 {White recaptures as the queen was under attack. We'll develop and block the e-file.} 7... Be7 8. cxd4 {White regains a pawn. And now we'll play d5 ourselves to stop white from harassing us with d5.} 8... d5 9. Bb5 {White pins us for one move. [%csl Rc6]} 9... O-O {And we get castled with an extra pawn!}) 6... d5 7. Bxd5 {White removes the defender of our knight. We'll recapture with the queen. [%csl Rd5]} (7. Nc3 {What about this insane move, leaving two pieces hanging? Let's just ignore the material and get more piece development in. Be6 blocks the rook's view of our king. [%csl Rc3,Rc4]} 7... Be6 8. Bxd5 {White can choose to remove a defender of our knight. We'll take on c3. [%csl Rc3]} (8. Nxe4 {White captures on e4 straight up. Let's take the pesky bishop instead of the knight. [%csl Rc4]} 8... dxc4 9. Neg5 {White pins our bishop because of the discovered attack from their rook, and mounts pressure on e6. [%cal Gg5e6] [%csl Ge8,Ge1]} 9... Be7 {Let's just develop to get out of danger. [%cal Ge7g5]}) 8... dxc3 9. Bxe4 {After White gets the piece back... We'll simply trade queens. [%csl Rd1]} 9... Qxd1 10. Rxd1 {White recaptures. We'll trade off pawns on b2. [%csl Rb2]} 10... cxb2 11. Bxb2 {White develops with a view to g7. So we'll block the diagonal! [%csl Rg7]} 11... f6 12. Re1 {White places their rook opposite our king, so... We'll remove our rook from the potential pin and defend our bishop with the king. [%csl Ge1,Re8,Re6]} 12... Kf7 13. Bc3 {White controls more squares with the bishop on c3.} 13... Bc5 {And we ourselves develop our bishop, meeting any rooks to b1 with ...Bb6. Then we'll look to activate the rooks. We are a safe pawn up. [%cal Gc5b6,Ga1b1,Gh8e8,Ga8d8]}) 7... Qxd5 8. Nc3 {White takes advantage of the pins! Our pawn is pinned and so is our knight on e4. But here we just fall back with the queen to d7. [%csl Ge1,Gd1,Re8,Rd5]} 8... Qd7 9. Nxe4 {Capturing with the knight sets up discoveries! Here we block the open file with our bishop. [%csl Ge1,Re8]} (9. Rxe4+ {Capturing with the rook gives a check right away. This is also met with Be7 blocking off the e-file. [%csl Re8]} 9... Be7 10. Nxd4 {White can capture, getting another piece into the center. And here we can strike at the central pieces with f5!} 10... f5 11. Rf4 {Rf4 gets out of danger but also removes the pin. Castling is now sensible. We also defend our f5 pawn with the rook. [%csl Rf5]} 11... O-O 12. Nxc6 {White exchanges a pair of knights. For what purpose? The rook looks misplaced on f4. We take back with the queen.} 12... Qxc6 13. Nd5 {After Nd5 threatening our bishop but most importantly a check on e7 winning our queen... [%csl Re7]} 13... Bd6 {We bring the bishop up one square, where we still defend e7. Next, we'll develop normally with ...Be6, and try to get a rook to the d- or the e-file. [%cal Gc8e6,Ga8d8,Gd8e8] [%csl Rf4,Rd5]}) 9... Be7 10. Bg5 {White pressurizes our bishop on e7 but... We can just get our king out of the e-file by castling. [%csl Ge1,Re7]} 10... O-O 11. Bxe7 {Once White chooses to trade... We'll just recapture with the queen. [%csl Re7]} 11... Qxe7 12. Nxd4 {White recovers another pawn.} 12... Bd7 {No issues, we just continue to develop. We have a bishop versus a knight in an open board. [%csl Gd7]}) 5... d5 6. Bb5 {White leaves c4 and pins our knight. And we leave f6 and settle our knight on e4. [%csl Rc6]} (6. exf6 {Should White take our piece... [%csl Rc4]} 6... dxc4 {We can take their bishop. We have an extra pawn and the bishop pair! [%csl Gf8,Gc8]}) 6... Ne4 7. Nxd4 {White mounts pressure on the pinned piece. But we develop without a care, playing the aggressive Bc5! [%csl Rc6]} 7... Bc5 8. Be3 {Be3 supports the d4 knight and counters the c5 bishop on the diagonal. Here we can safely castle.} (8. Nxc6 {In this variation White tries to win material. But we can take on f2 with check and make White lose castling rights! [%csl Rf2]} 8... Bxf2+ 9. Kf1 {White moves out of check. Now we recapture on c6.} 9... bxc6 10. Bxc6+ {White checks us and attacks our rook. We slide over to f8. The trick is that White cannot take our rook because we've got 11...Ba6+ in reserve. [%csl Re8,Ra8]} 10... Kf8 11. c4 {That's why White tries to be clever and blocks off the diagonal from our bishop. In which case we can be persistent in attacking! The queen comes to h4!} 11... Qh4 12. Bxa8 {Capturing our rook still isn't possible... Because we retreat the bishop, threatening checkmate on f2. [%cal Gf2c5] [%csl Rf2]} 12... Bc5 13. Qf3 {White attempts to defend, but...} 13... Bg4 {Bg4 removes the queen from the defence of f2, and things are getting nasty. [%csl Rf2]}) (8. O-O {White can choose to castle as the knight is sufficiently defended. We'll castle as well!} 8... O-O 9. Nxc6 {White can go for the pawn here, with a tempo on our queen. We'll recapture with a tempo on their bishop. [%csl Rc6,Rd8]} (9. Bxc6 {White can opt for taking with the bishop. We'll recapture.} 9... bxc6 10. Be3 {White supports their central knight. We slide the queen over where it defends c6 but also attacks e5. [%csl Gd4,Re5,Rc6]} 10... Qe8 11. f3 {If White is tired of our presence on e4... We can retreat, banking on the e-pawn being pinned! [%csl Ge8,Re3]} 11... Nf6 12. f4 {If f4, defending the advanced pawn...} 12... Ng4 {Jumping into g4, attacking the bishop, comes with great effect. [%csl Re3]}) 9... bxc6 10. Bxc6 {The bishop recaptures, attacking our rook. But we can counterattack their rook! [%csl Ra8]} 10... Ba6 11. Qxd5 {I suppose White could take the d-pawn as they've got sufficient pressure. But we can take that rook. [%csl Rf1]} (11. Bxa8 {White can go for the exchange of the rooks. So we acquiesce and take a rook ourselves.} 11... Bxf1 12. Kxf1 {Now the king recaptures, leaving White with pressure on d5 still. [%csl Rd5]} 12... Qh4 {But we can just bring the queen out and attack f2 and h2. We are much better developed than White. [%csl Gh4,Ge4,Gc5,Rf2]}) 11... Bxf1 12. Qxe4 {White could delay capturing the rook by taking our knight first. [%csl Ra8,Rf1]} 12... Bb5 {But here we've got the awesome retreating move Bb5, giving our bishop in view of a check on d1 with our queen, followed by capturing on c1. [%csl Rd1]}) 8... O-O 9. Nxc6 {White goes for the pawn. We'll recapture.} 9... bxc6 10. Bxc5 {White trades off bishops before capturing the pawn. We'll accept the trade.} 10... Nxc5 11. Bxc6 {Now white takes on c6, forking our rook and pawn. But we'll step out of the way, attacking b2. [%csl Ra8,Rd5]} 11... Rb8 12. Qxd5 {After the queen takes our pawn...} 12... Qe7 {We come to e7, defending our knight. In the following moves we'll be able to bring our rooks to the open files and wreak havoc on White's position, despite being a pawn down. [%cal Ge7c5]} *

