{In this study we'll go over some deviations which aren't particularly good for White, but you might indeed see them in an actual game, especially at lower levels. We're talking about move 4 choices by White which we can exploit. These moves are 4.0-0/c3/Nc3. So let's begin with 1.e4 e5!}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 {White attacks e5. We'll develop and defend it. [%csl Re5]} 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 {The bishop is taken out in standard Italian fashion. And we'll develop and attack e4. [%csl Re4]} 3... Nf6 4. O-O {What if White just castles, contending that perhaps Re1 after we capture on e4 will give them sufficient counterplay? This move is played 10% of the time, which is around 7.5 million games. We are happy to test our their idea! We'll capture the pawn. [%csl Re4]} (4. c3 {What about c3, preparing d4? It's only played 4% of the time, and that's because... We can simply take the unprotected e-pawn. [%csl Re4]} 4... Nxe4 5. O-O {White can pretend they'll be dynamic and get activity for the pawn. We've got to be used to this move by now, attacking the bishop and defending our knight on e4. [%csl Rc4]} (5. d4 {White can continue with their original idea of d4! Let's play the same old move we're used to, attacking the bishop and defending our knight.} 5... d5 6. Bb5 {White pins us. Let's take once on d4 and preserve our extra pawn. [%csl Rc6]} 6... exd4 7. Qxd4 {White can take with the queen as our knight is pinned. We'll unpin it, while making sure d5 is still protected. [%csl Rc6]} 7... Qd7 {We unpin it.} 8. Bxc6 {White takes the attacker. We'll recapture with the queen.} 8... Qxc6 9. O-O {White castles. But before we can develop our f8 bishop to castle ourselves, we need to cover g7. Hence f6. [%csl Rg7]} 9... f6 10. Qd3 {White gets away from the d4 square, where our bishop to c5 could have attacked the queen. We'll develop it anyway.} 10... Be7 11. Nd4 {Our queen comes under attack. But we can retreat to d7. [%csl Rc6]} 11... Qd7 12. Re1 {Re1 places the rook opposite the king for one move. [%csl Ge1,Re8]} 12... O-O {We secure our king. In the future, ...c5 and ...c4 might be in the cards. We'll look to improve the position of the dark-squared bishop, probably to c5, and develop the rest of the pieces. We've also maintained the extra pawn. [%cal Gc7c5,Gc5c4,Ge7c5]}) 5... d5 6. Bb5 {White moves over, pinning our knight. We'll develop and defend e5. [%csl Rc6,Re5]} 6... Bd6 7. Re1 {White brings the rook opposite our king. We'll just get out of danger and castle. [%csl Ge1,Re8]} 7... O-O 8. d3 {After d3, removing our knight from e4... We can retreat to the active c5 square. [%csl Re4]} 8... Nc5 9. Bxc6 {White removes a defender of e5. We'll recapture.} 9... bxc6 10. Nxe5 {White takes on e5. We'll bring our queen to an attacking position in front of the white king.} 10... Qh4 11. Nf3 {Retreating attacks our queen and defends h2. [%csl Rh2]} 11... Qh5 {But we maintain the queen on the h-file to always keep an eye on h2, while threatening Bg4. [%cal Gc8g4]}) (4. Nc3 {What about this logical move, defending e4? It's played 16% of the time. We can take it anyways! [%csl Re4]} 4... Nxe4 5. Nxe4 {Once White captures, we've got the famous "center fork trick."} 5... d5 {d5 ensures we'll regain the piece, while being a useful move to develop our queen and light-squared bishop. [%csl Re4,Rc4]}) 4... Nxe4 5. Re1 {White can bring the rook to the e-file, opposite our king. Expect to see this move about half the time. The response to this is to defend the knight and attack the bishop at once!} (5. Bd5 {Now this is the top computer move, forking both knights, one of which (the knight on c6) is the defender of e5. Even though it's only played 4% of the time, you might just face it against a prepared opponent. We can retreat, attacking the bishop. [%csl Re4,Rc6]} 5... Nf6 6. Bxc6 {The bishop takes the defender of e5. We'll recapture away from the center to open up our queen and light-squared bishop.} 6... dxc6 7. Nxe5 {White regains the pawn. But we will develop with a tempo on the knight.} 7... Bd6 8. d4 {White defends it. We'll get safely castled.} 8... O-O 9. Bg5 {Play could continue with white pinning us. Here we can ask the bishop a question. [%csl Rf6]} 9... h6 10. Bh4 {But they can choose to maintain the pin. We'll add another attacker to the knight on e5. [%csl Re5]} 10... Re8 11. Nc3 {White develops the knight with the idea of jumping to e4 and applying pressure on our pinned knight. [%cal Gc3e4] [%csl Rf6]} 11... Bf5 {We therefore develop the bishop and cover e4. We have a comfortable position. [%cal Gf5e4]}) 5... d5 6. Bb5 {Once White pins us... We can develop, converging on f2! [%csl Rc6,Rf2]} 6... Bc5 7. d4 {White blocks the diagonal and our attack on f2. I don't know if you're counting, but this is a second pawn for us. [%csl Rd4]} 7... exd4 8. Nbd2 {White pressurizes our knight on e4. Here I suggest castling and getting to safety, as we'll already up two pawns. We can give one up for the sake of safety and development. [%csl Re4]} 8... O-O 9. Nxe4 {White initiates the sequence that wins a pawn back. We'll recapture.} 9... dxe4 10. Rxe4 {And White gets one pawn back.} 10... Qd5 {We can develop the queen with a tempo. We're a pawn up with a better position. [%cal Gd5e4] [%csl Re4]} *
